Original commit body from 28f3ef87f687c9614402284bba99492afede5e8e:

py.py's terminal bare-Name arm gated EMISSION on BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES --
frozenset({"open"}), a set whose purpose is receiver typing for I/O
classification. So the permitting condition was "this builtin is a catalogued
I/O primitive", and print/len/input/eval/getattr emitted nothing, as did every
unresolvable bare name.

The cost is not about I/O. ADR-0017 section 4 holds a TERMINATING summary for
print under both keys and could never apply, because the callee never reached
callees_at, so the section 3a walk recorded an ESCAPE where it had an
accounted-for step -- 50.0% of section 3a escape sites, the largest cause.
Verified Python-specific rather than assumed: 43 analyzers call
make_unresolved_edge and py.py called it zero times.

The fix splits two questions one set was answering. "Is this a real builtin?"
is a fact about the language, now read from builtins itself. "May an unbound
name be trusted to carry a catalogued receiver type?" stays with
BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES, unchanged and default-deny.

A/B, baseline asserted by grepping the runtime file, every arm file checked
non-empty before restore: hypergumbo-core 74,641 -> 87,834 (+13,193), pretix
92,344 -> 100,703 (+8,359), ZERO lost in both.

Reading rows back against source corrected the fix twice, and neither defect
showed in the totals. The guard first saw only the caller's own frame, so
pretix's function(request, ...) -- an enclosing decorator's parameter called
from a nested wrapper -- was admitted; a closure-captured open would then have
minted a fabricated builtin, the StreamWriter defect by another route. Now the
union over every LEGB frame: pretix's unresolvable bare names went 22 -> 0,
builtin counts unmoved. And excluding dunders on taste pushed __import__ (8 call
sites in hypergumbo-core) into external -- a sentinel for a name the language
defines.

Direction on the walk, stated because it is not the flattering one: a
confirmation cannot be lost (return True short-circuits at taint.py:3598), but a
new callee with no terminating summary turns "not an escape" into an escape, so
this moves False -> None only -- fewer refutations, never a suppressed finding.
Only builtins.print carries a summary today, so 459 of 13,193 new edges are
consultable; summaries for the common pure builtins are filed as a prerequisite
for granting section 3a removal authority.

